    {
      "id": "grammar-tener-que-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "tener que + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone has to do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of tener, then que, then the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The dictionary form is the verb's basic, unchanged form ending in -ar, -er, or -ir: hablar means 'to speak,' comer means 'to eat,' and salir means 'to leave.' Only tener changes for the person and time. Tengo que means 'I have to,' tienes que means 'you have to' for one familiar person, and tiene que means 'he or she has to' or formal 'you have to.' Que and the final action verb stay unchanged: tengo que salir, tienes que salir, tiene que salir.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Tengo que salir temprano.",
        "en": "I have to leave early."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A1",
        "grammar",
        "obligation",
        "verb pattern"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-relative-superlative",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "el, la, los o las + nombre opcional + más + descripción",
      "questionEn": "How do you use el más or la más to say that something has the most of a quality?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching word for 'the,' name the thing if needed, then add más and the describing word.",
      "noteEn": "You may leave out the noun, the name of the person or thing, when it is already clear. El, la, los, or las matches the thing, and the describing word also matches when its form changes: la calle más larga, or la más larga when calle is understood. Más stays unchanged and marks the highest degree within the relevant group. The grammar name for this highest-degree description is a superlative.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Es la calle más larga.",
        "en": "It is the longest street."
      },
      "tags": [
        "grammar",
        "superlative",
        "comparison",
        "A2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-es-adjective-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "es + descripción + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you use es to say that an activity is easy, difficult, useful, or similar?",
      "answerEn": "Use es, then a describing word, then the activity verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "The dictionary form ends in -ar, -er, or -ir: esperar, aprender, salir. It names the activity in general rather than saying who does it. Spanish needs no separate word for the English it in 'It is difficult to wait.' For a past evaluation, es can change to era or fue; the activity still keeps its dictionary form.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Es difícil esperar.",
        "en": "It is difficult to wait."
      },
      "tags": [
        "grammar",
        "evaluation",
        "infinitive",
        "A2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-poder-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "poder + verbo en forma de diccionario",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone can, could, or may do something?",
      "answerEn": "Use a form of poder for the person and situation, then keep the action verb in its unchanged dictionary form.",
      "noteEn": "Poder changes to forms such as puedo, puedes, puede, podemos, and pueden for present ability or possibility. Podría can present a possible or imagined option. The following action keeps its -ar, -er, or -ir ending.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Podemos entrar ahora.",
        "en": "We can enter now."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A1",
        "grammar",
        "ability",
        "possibility",
        "verb pattern"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "grammar-pronoun-attached-to-infinitive",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "verbo en forma de diccionario + pronombre unido",
      "questionEn": "Where can a short object word such as me, te, lo, or la go with an unchanged action verb?",
      "answerEn": "Attach it to the end of the verb and write the result as one word.",
      "noteEn": "An unchanged dictionary-form verb ends in -ar, -er, or -ir. Small words naming who or what receives the action can attach directly: conocer and te become conocerte, and ayudar and me become ayudarme.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Quiero ayudarte.",
        "en": "I want to help you."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A2",
        "grammar",
        "object pronouns",
        "infinitive"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-al-fin",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "al fin",
      "questionEn": "How do you express relief that something has finally happened?",
      "answerEn": "Use al fin to mean “finally” or “at last.”",
      "noteEn": "This fixed expression often highlights that a wait, delay, or difficulty has ended. It can appear before or after the action.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Al fin llegó el autobús.",
        "en": "At last, the bus arrived."
      },
      "tags": [
        "A2",
        "expression",
        "time",
        "relief"
      ]
    }
  ],
